Independent T2 review — 3d10b4b19c31b04b8a4ff617102d480431d026c4

Verdict: request changes. Three major defects and two minor defects were reproduced. T1 landing alone does not address the identified code paths. Fix these and run the combined T1/T2 gates before merge. No runtime unsoundness was demonstrated.

The criteria were recorded in criteria.md before reading the compiler diff. Review scope was git diff refs/heads/redesign...HEAD in lane/t2. No tracked files were edited and no commit was made. The existing release binary was verified current by rebuilding through the required heavy/direnv wrapper. All custom source, scripts, output and reports are in /tmp/review-t2/.

Criteria and coverage

Types must continue to denote sets of values, with principal inference subject only to the specified completion equations. Value type syntax must respect ordinary scope, the value restriction, scheme instantiation, monomorphic sharing, early-context bans and place restrictions. Permanent aliases must be closed, stable observations, with all demands checked against one snapshot. Pattern matching must use normalized type members, retain shared variables under skips, never choose generalized instances, and never discard a union alternative. Expression SCCs, generalization, runtime needs and evaluation order must remain separate from type dependencies. Foreign permanent reads must pass the whole-source closure gate. Exports, package interfaces and language-server queries must reflect the completed result without leaking private nominal types. Schema libraries must remain ordinary i code.

| Hard case | Scratch projects / evidence | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Value/alias cycle and mixed value/type cycle | cycle_alias, cycle_mixed | E0530, chain and correct annotation location |
| Mutual top-level recursion and internal type edge | mutual_ok, mutual_type | Ordinary recursion accepted; type edge rejected E0530 |
| Local shadowing, entered inner binder, pending header | shadow, shadow_reserved, pending | Expected acceptance / E0536 |
| Generalized reads, unsolved weak parts, invariant generalization | generalized_alias, generalized_anno, unfilled, invariant_general | Fresh annotation instances; permanent generalized/unfilled reads rejected |
| Weak source completion and earlier weak dependencies | weak_alias, prior_weak, earlier_constraints | Own alias closes Int; earlier-open cases reject E0534 |
| Mismatch, extra union alternatives, repeated binder | mismatch, extra_union, duplicate | E0535 / E0208 |
| Nested, partial and shared-skip patterns | nested, partial, shared_skip | Accepted; nominal/tuple extraction works |
| Constant subtrees and nonlinear head alias | exact_good, exact_bad, repeated_formal, alias_repeated_nested | Expected success / E0535 / E0532 |
| Equivalent bounds and qualified alias pattern | same_type_explicit, union_samehead_direct, qualified_alias_pattern | Findings 1 and 2 |
| Open/closed foreign reads; alias-only consumer | foreign_open, foreign_weak, foreign_closed, foreign_alias | Whole-source gate enforced; exported closed alias works |
| Source order | order/0 through order/119; fixed_later, fixed_reordered | All 120 permutations identical after sorting --types; fixed later conflict rejects in both orders |
| Export privacy / early contexts | privacy, early_field, early_external | E0227 / E0531 |
| Bad alias plus independent error / queries | recovery, batch_recovery; recovery/lsp.json | Independent E0501 remains; failed binder hover retains written pattern |
| Query source/binder/head navigation | nested/lsp.json | Source/binder navigation and outline work; nested head missing (finding 5) |
| Dependent packages and schema motivation | pkg_lib -> pkg_app -> pkg_client | Ordinary imported Schema builder, local extraction, exported User alias and private source work across package boundaries |
| Runtime alias tests, instance freshness, inferred-vs-known, schema | runtime/test/main.i, runtime/o0.txt, runtime/o2.txt | Four tests pass at both --opt 0 and --opt 2 |
| Scale | chain*, valuechain*, ordinarychain*, reversechain*, timing.json | Finding 3 |

All named projects were run with target/release/i check --project /tmp/review-t2/NAME --types unless the row specifies LSP/runtime. The exact sources and check.txt output are retained. results.jsonl, more.jsonl and finalprobes.jsonl record the initial matrices. The 'expected' entries in those exploratory logs were hypotheses, not findings: in particular foreign_open's generalized skipped argument legitimately gets E0533 under design R7, distinct from E0534 for a weak open argument. weak_anno is a wording ambiguity discussed below, not a confirmed compiler defect.

Findings

1. Major — alias-pattern matching rejects equivalent same-head lower bounds.

Reproducer: same_type_explicit/src/main.i

```i
Box(A) { Box(A) }
left: Int = 1
right: Int = 2
v = True |> { True -> Box(left)  False -> Box(right) }
Box(Item) = v
```

Expected: success, Item = Int. Both alternatives have the same sole type constructor; the written Int inputs also make the expectation independent of T1 literal narrowing.
Actual: --types prints v : Box(Int), but E0535 at the source v says it does not match the alias pattern. Merely annotating v with Box(Int) accepts. A whole-value alias of the unannotated source also succeeds. With Int/String alternatives, a direct match fails whereas wrapping the same match in a function and calling it succeeds.
Evidence: run and traced in code. crates/ilang-check/src/types/value_types.rs:225–239 deduplicates raw Ty nodes and requires exactly one raw bound, rather than normalizing their union into members. Distinct argument variables make equivalent Box bounds different raw nodes. This violates spec §§4.3–4.4 and design P4's 'exactly one normalized member'. Any repair must preserve original argument sharing and the skip rule.

2. Major — qualified aliases to closed named types are rejected as pattern parts.

Reproducer: qualified_alias_closed/ (i.toml: name = "review")

```i
// src/lib.i
(Scalar)
Scalar = Int

// src/main.i
(review/lib)
Box(A) {Box(A)}
v: Box(Int) = Box(1)
Box(lib.Scalar) = v
```

Expected: success. Scalar transparently expands to Int; the qualified pattern checks that closed part and binds no name.
Actual: E0532 on the outer Box: 'an alias-pattern head must expand to one named type or tuple'.
Evidence: run and traced. resolve.rs:1291–1295 accepts only Named/Tuple from projectable_body, but a parameter-free alias body is represented as Exact. This rejects a permitted transparent alias before matching. Spec §4.3 explicitly permits qualified named patterns and aliases expanding to one named type or tuple; design P2–P3 applies the same rules to nested aliases. It must still reject aliases whose expanded head is a forbidden form.

3. Major — completion repeatedly expands aliases, with severe declaration-order-sensitive cost.

Concrete complete sources: chain512/src/main.i, reversechain512/src/main.i, valuechain2048/src/main.i. These are shallow alias chains, all resolving to the one scalar Int:

```i
v: Int = 1
A0 = v
A1 = A0
// Every successive Ai = A(i-1), through A511.
x: A511 = 2
```

The reverse fixture writes A511 through A1 before A0, without changing any dependency or type. The complete generated .i files contain every definition; reverse_scale.py and finalprobes.py generate them.

Expected: completion in dependency order with reused completed results; declaration order should not add repeated whole-graph work. This is not a recursively growing output type.
Actual (release, three samples, shared machine): 512 forward median 0.526 s; 512 reverse 4.633 s. Forward 1,024: 1.472 s; 2,048: 5.881 s. The 2,048 ordinary-alias control with A0 = Int takes 0.020 s. The 4,096 value-dependent chain exceeded a 12-second watchdog; this is a timeout observation, not proof of nontermination. Earlier reverse-size samples 128/256/512 took 0.094/0.592/4.201 s.
Evidence: executed, samples in timing.json and reverse_scale.jsonl. Code cause: assemble_aliases (value_types.rs:1163) repeatedly scans all definitions; value_dependent traversals are rebuilt, and alias_shown:1272 recursively expands written alias bodies instead of reusing already completed alias bodies. Reverse source order permits only one chain node to finish per scan. Memoize dependency summaries and completed parameterized bodies and schedule ready aliases from their dependency graph. The implementer's std timing does not exercise this feature-heavy case.

4. Minor — the fixation note identifies another value's alias.

Reproducer: fix_location/src/main.i

```i
Cell(A) {Cell(get:()->A,set:(A)->Nil)}
cell(A)=(x:A){Cell(get:(){x},set:(y:A){Nil})}
put(A)=(c:Cell(A),x:A){c.set(x)}
(a,b)=(cell(1),cell("s"))
First=a
Second=b
later=(){put(b,1)}
```

Expected: reject Int into b's String parameter, with the fixation note at Second=b.
Actual: correct E0501, but 'this type was fixed here: review/main:5:7' points to First=a.
Evidence: run and traced. value_types.rs:660–667 selects one first permanent site for the entire group, then attaches it to every equation. Spec §4.7 and design R5 require the place that fixed the affected part. Keep provenance per demanded variable/equation, including shared-variable cases.

5. Minor — nested alias-pattern heads have no hover or definition target.

Reproducer: nested/src/main.i

```i
Pair(A,B) = (A,B)
Box(A) { Box(A) }
v = (Box(1),"s")
Pair(Box(Item),_) = v
x: Item = 2
```

Expected: hover/go-to-definition on Box in Pair(Box(Item),_) identifies the Box type on line 2.
Actual: both JSON-RPC requests return result: null. Item hover is 'Item = Int'; its definition, source-v navigation and outline work.
Evidence: actual i lsp session, raw messages in nested/lsp.json, reproducible with lsp.py. query.rs:603–610 visits only the outer pattern head and source. It never walks nested TypePattern heads; the HIR also does not retain their individual name spans. This leaves design X4's pattern-head navigation incomplete.

Interpretations and spec text

The interpretation matrix in tests/t2-stages.md mostly quotes the governing design correctly. The own-module partial-demand/foreign whole-closure distinction, fresh generalized instances, retained weak sharing, ordinary lookup, private constructor behavior, inferred-versus-known distinction, and closed package alias bodies agree with the cited sentences. The alternate pick fixture must actually preserve distinct variables to test the place rule; the record is correct about that. The ordinary generic setter helper still exercises invariant constraints while avoiding the acknowledged pre-T1 known-field issue. Neither adjustment excuses the defects above.

The claims of completed semantic/query coverage are too broad: P4 normalization and P2 qualified constant aliases are missing, X4 only covers outer heads, and the standard-library performance run misses completion chains. 'No controlling source was overturned' should not be interpreted as evidence that these implementation details follow the spec.

Two normative passages need clarification:

* Spec §4.3 says every pattern is checked 'after its sources and alias dependencies are complete', while its skip bullet says 'After source completion, the whole source type must first be closed'. Read alone these can incorrectly prohibit the required local partial codec extraction. §4.7 and design R2/P4/R7 distinguish demands processed AT the source's own completion from new permanent reads AFTER it. State that distinction explicitly in §4.3.
* Spec §4.7 says 'All value-type demands written in its own module are collected before inference' and 'At this point their weak parts are fixed', while its annotation bullet says reading an unfixed completed top-level part is an error. The compiler only collects permanent alias/pattern reads as fixation demands. In weak_anno, id=(x){x}; v=id(1); x:v=2 fails E0534; adding Read=v makes it work. Clarify whether annotation-only demands trigger fixation, or explicitly restrict this collection rule to permanent reads. Because the sources admit competing readings, this review does not present that example as a confirmed implementation bug.

The quoted spec obligations definitively violated by findings 1, 2 and 4 are, respectively: the exactly-one-member matching rule plus union normalization; the transparent-alias/qualified-pattern rule; and the note 'at the place that fixed it'. Design O1/P5's memoized dependency ordering and X4's pattern-head targets supply the additional requirements for findings 3 and 5.

Verification and merge gate

Executed afresh, with every cargo command using the required wrapper:
* cargo build --release -p ilang-cli --locked
* cargo test -p ilang-check --test value_types --lib --locked: 24 integration tests and 5 solver tests pass.
* cargo test -p ilang-cli value_type_interfaces --locked: all 3 interface tests pass (other suites filtered, not claimed rerun).
* Custom checker programs above, 120 declaration permutations, real LSP requests, dependent-package fixture, release timings, and 4 custom runtime tests at each optimization level.

No accepted-program runtime failure, silent alias mutation, or declaration-order-dependent acceptance was demonstrated. These negative results are limited to the tested programs, not a proof of soundness/principality. The full original dev-verify/browser/WAT gates were not independently rerun.

Do not merge merely because T1 lands. Fix the two valid-program rejections and the completion scale problem first; correct the two tooling/diagnostic defects as well. After rebasing, rerun T1/T2/K1 tests, exact literal preservation in closure and pattern normalization, >12-literal interface changes, whole-expanded place checks, ordinary group-check-before-publication behavior, and runtime aliases at O0/O2. The full record-shaped Schema(User,_) use remains T3's integration gate: T2's ordinary tuple/scalar schema fixture succeeds, including across packages, without special library rules.
